151:     /**
152:      * <p>Main constructor for a {@link FigClass}.</p>
153:      *
154:      * <p>Parent {@link FigNodeModelElement} will have created the main box
155:      *   {@link #_bigPort} and its name {@link #_name} and stereotype (@link
156:      *   #_stereo}. This constructor creates a box for the attributes and
157:      *   operations.</p>
158:      *
159:      * <p>The properties of all these graphic elements are adjusted
160:      *   appropriately. The main boxes are all filled and have outlines.</p>
161:      *
162:      * <p>For reasons I don't understand the stereotype is created in a box
163:      *   with lines. So we have to created a blanking rectangle to overlay the
164:      *   bottom line, and avoid four compartments showing.</p>
165:      *
166:      * <p>There is some complex logic to allow for the possibility that
167:      *   stereotypes may not be displayed (unlike operations and attributes
168:      *   this is not a standard thing for UML). Some care is needed to ensure
169:      *   that additional space is not added, each time a stereotyped class is
170:      *   loaded.</p>
171:      *
172:      * <p>There is a particular problem when loading diagrams with stereotyped
173:      *   classes. Because we create a FigClass indicating the stereotype is not
174:      *   displayed, we then add extra space for such classes when they are
175:      *   first rendered. This ought to be fixed by correctly saving the class
176:      *   dimensions, but that needs more work. The solution here is to use a
177:      *   simple flag to indicate the FigClass has just been created.</p>
178:      *
179:      * <p><em>Warning</em>. Much of the graphics positioning is hard coded. The
180:      *   overall figure is placed at location (10,10). The name compartment (in
181:      *   the parent {@link FigNodeModelElement} is 21 pixels high. The
182:      *   stereotype compartment is created 15 pixels high in the parent, but we
183:      *   change it to 19 pixels, 1 more than ({@link #STEREOHEIGHT} here. The
184:      *   attribute and operations boxes are created at 19 pixels, 2 more than
185:      *   {@link #ROWHEIGHT}.</p>
186:      *
187:      * <p>CAUTION: This constructor (with no arguments) is the only one
188:      *   that does enableSizeChecking(false), all others must set it true.
189:      *   This is because this constructor is the only one called when loading
190:      *   a project. In this case, the parsed size must be maintained.</p>
191:      */
192: 
193:     public FigClass() {
194: 
195:         // Set name box. Note the upper line will be blanked out if there is
196:         // eventually a stereotype above.
197:         _name.setLineWidth(1);
198:         _name.setFilled(true);
199: 
200:         // this rectangle marks the attribute section; all attributes are inside it
201:         _attrBigPort =
202:             new FigRect(10, 30, 60, ROWHEIGHT + 2, Color.black, Color.white);
203:         _attrBigPort.setFilled(true);
204:         _attrBigPort.setLineWidth(1);
205: 
206:         // Attributes inside. First one is the attribute box itself.
207:         _attrVec = new FigGroup();
208:         _attrVec.setFilled(true);
209:         _attrVec.setLineWidth(1);
210:         _attrVec.addFig(_attrBigPort);
211: 
212:         // this rectangle marks the operation section; all operations are inside it
213:         _operBigPort =
214:             new FigRect(
215:                 10,
216:                 31 + ROWHEIGHT,
217:                 60,
218:                 ROWHEIGHT + 2,
219:                 Color.black,
220:                 Color.white);
221:         _operBigPort.setFilled(true);
222:         _operBigPort.setLineWidth(1);
223: 
224:         _operVec = new FigGroup();
225:         _operVec.setFilled(true);
226:         _operVec.setLineWidth(1);
227:         _operVec.addFig(_operBigPort);
228: 
229:         // Set properties of the stereotype box. Make it 1 pixel higher than
230:         // before, so it overlaps the name box, and the blanking takes out both
231:         // lines. Initially not set to be displayed, but this will be changed
232:         // when we try to render it, if we find we have a stereotype.
233:         _stereo.setExpandOnly(true);
234:         _stereo.setFilled(true);
235:         _stereo.setLineWidth(1);
236:         _stereo.setEditable(false);
237:         _stereo.setHeight(STEREOHEIGHT + 1);
238:         // +1 to have 1 pixel overlap with _name
239:         _stereo.setDisplayed(false);
240: 
241:         // A thin rectangle to overlap the boundary line between stereotype
242:         // and name. This is just 2 pixels high, and we rely on the line
243:         // thickness, so the rectangle does not need to be filled. Whether to
244:         // display is linked to whether to display the stereotype.
245:         _stereoLineBlinder =
246:             new FigRect(11, 10 + STEREOHEIGHT, 58, 2, Color.white, Color.white);
247:         _stereoLineBlinder.setLineWidth(1);
248:         _stereoLineBlinder.setDisplayed(false);
249: 
250:         // Mark this as newly created. This is to get round the problem with
251:         // creating figs for loaded classes that had stereotypes. They are
252:         // saved with their dimensions INCLUDING the stereotype, but since we
253:         // pretend the stereotype is not visible, we add height the first time
254:         // we render such a class. This is a complete fudge, and really we
255:         // ought to address how class objects with stereotypes are saved. But
256:         // that will be hard work.
257:         _newlyCreated = true;
258: 
259:         // Put all the bits together, suppressing bounds calculations until
260:         // we're all done for efficiency.
261:         enableSizeChecking(false);
262:         suppressCalcBounds = true;
263:         addFig(_bigPort);
264:         // addFig(_namespace);
265:         addFig(_stereo);
266:         addFig(_name);
267:         addFig(_stereoLineBlinder);
268:         addFig(_attrVec);
269:         addFig(_operVec);
270:         suppressCalcBounds = false;
271: 
272:         // Set the bounds of the figure to the total of the above (hardcoded)
273:         setBounds(10, 10, 60, 22 + 2 * ROWHEIGHT);
274:     }
